* Two-stage Miller opamp closed in unity gain
* Feedback goes to the n1 gate, not the n2 gate. In the opamp_miller
* topology the mirror-side input is the INVERTING one: raising it pulls n1
* down, which drives vo1i up through p4 and turns the p6 output device off.
* Closing the loop on the other input gives positive feedback and rails the
* output in both engines, which is how this polarity was caught.
* The inverting input is the output node, so the operating point is a
* property of the closed loop rather than of any swept source. There is no
* .ic card: a transient on this deck must start from a computed DC operating
* point, which is the cold-start path every uic transient in the suite skips.
<MODEL_SETUP>
.temp <TEMP>
Vdd vdd 0 <VDD>
Vbn vbn 0 <VBN>
Vin vin 0 <BUFFER_IN>
<N_PREFIX>n1 n1 vout vtail 0 <N_DEVICE>
<N_PREFIX>n2 vo1i vin vtail 0 <N_DEVICE>
<P_PREFIX>p3 n1 n1 vdd vdd <P_DEVICE>
<P_PREFIX>p4 vo1i n1 vdd vdd <P_DEVICE>
<N_PREFIX>n5 vtail vbn 0 0 <N_DEVICE>
<P_PREFIX>p6 vout vo1i vdd vdd <P_DEVICE>
<N_PREFIX>n7 vout vbn 0 0 <N_DEVICE>
Cc vo1i vout <CC>
CL vout 0 <CL>
<ANALYSIS>
.end
